1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,923
In August 1945, inside a top-secret wooden laboratory in the remote canyons

2
00:00:04,923 --> 00:00:08,614
of New Mexico, scientists unpacked a heavy wooden crate.

3
00:00:08,614 --> 00:00:11,896
Nestled inside sat a fourteen-pound sphere of shiny,

4
00:00:11,896 --> 00:00:15,588
silver-gray metal. It was smaller than a bowling ball,

5
00:00:15,588 --> 00:00:19,690
yet contained enough destructive potential to incinerate an entire metropolis.

6
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:24,765
This was Rufus, the third plutonium pit engineered by the Manhattan Project.

7
00:00:24,765 --> 00:00:29,440
It was scheduled to be loaded into a B-29 bomber and dropped

8
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:34,116
onto Kokura or Tokyo. But when Japan surrendered unconditionally on August 15th,

9
00:00:34,116 --> 00:00:38,791
Rufus was abruptly canceled, leaving the world's deadliest weapon stranded on a

10
00:00:38,791 --> 00:00:39,570
laboratory workbench.

11
00:00:39,970 --> 00:00:44,532
Instead of being dismantled, the plutonium core was shipped to Omega Site,

12
00:00:44,532 --> 00:00:48,715
an isolated cluster of wooden barracks hidden deep inside Pajarito Canyon.

13
00:00:48,715 --> 00:00:51,376
Here, far from town and prying eyes,

14
00:00:51,376 --> 00:00:55,938
a handful of young physicists were tasked with testing the outer boundaries

15
00:00:55,938 --> 00:00:58,980
of nuclear criticality with virtually zero radiation shielding.

16
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:03,123
Plutonium-239 is an unstable element. At any given second,

17
00:01:03,123 --> 00:01:06,866
spontaneous fission releases high-speed neutrons. In a bare sphere,

18
00:01:06,866 --> 00:01:10,609
most of these neutrons escape harmlessly into the room.

19
00:01:10,609 --> 00:01:14,768
But if escaping neutrons are reflected back into the core,

20
00:01:14,768 --> 00:01:19,758
each fission triggers two more, sparking an uncontrollable exponential cascade known as

21
00:01:19,758 --> 00:01:20,590
prompt criticality.

22
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:25,233
The war had ended, but scientific curiosity turned recklessly casual.

23
00:01:25,233 --> 00:01:29,486
There were no remote mechanical arms, no lead-glass observation bunkers,

24
00:01:29,486 --> 00:01:34,590
and no emergency fail-safes. Brilliant young physicists moved deadly nuclear cores with

25
00:01:34,590 --> 00:01:39,693
their bare hands, smoking cigarettes and laughing as radiation counters clicked like

26
00:01:39,693 --> 00:01:41,820
agitated rattlesnakes on wooden tables.

27
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:46,420
The legendary physicist Richard Feynman was utterly appalled by the sheer recklessness

28
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:48,870
of these experiments. He issued a grim,

29
00:01:48,870 --> 00:01:53,070
unforgettable warning to his colleagues, saying that testing how close you could

30
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:57,270
push a nuclear core to explosion was exactly like tickling the tail

31
00:01:57,270 --> 00:01:58,670
of a sleeping dragon.

32
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:03,447
The purpose of these experiments was to minimize the mass of plutonium

33
00:02:03,447 --> 00:02:07,825
required for future atomic weapons. By surrounding the subcritical pit with dense

34
00:02:07,825 --> 00:02:11,473
neutron-reflecting materials, escaping neutrons would bounce back into the core,

35
00:02:11,473 --> 00:02:15,850
artificially tricking the plutonium into behaving as if it were a much

36
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:16,580
larger mass.

37
00:02:16,990 --> 00:02:21,824
For their first reflector material, the Los Alamos team chose tungsten carbide—an

38
00:02:21,824 --> 00:02:26,657
extraordinarily dense, heavy gray ceramic. Each brick weighed nine point seven pounds.

39
00:02:26,657 --> 00:02:31,491
By stacking these dense blocks one by one around the plutonium core,

40
00:02:31,491 --> 00:02:36,324
scientists could measure neutron multiplication rising closer and closer toward the lethal

41
00:02:36,324 --> 00:02:37,130
red line.

42
00:02:37,530 --> 00:02:40,157
With every brick added to the stack,

43
00:02:40,157 --> 00:02:44,661
the physics inside the sphere intensified. The clicks from the boron trifluoride

44
00:02:44,661 --> 00:02:48,789
radiation counters steadily quickened from an occasional crackle into an urgent,

45
00:02:48,789 --> 00:02:53,293
buzzing hum. The scientists relied entirely on audio feedback and visual needle

46
00:02:53,293 --> 00:02:55,920
deflections to know when to stop stacking.

47
00:02:56,310 --> 00:03:00,125
In reactor physics, the multiplication factor k dictates life and death.

48
00:03:00,125 --> 00:03:03,247
At k less than one, the reaction dies out.

49
00:03:03,247 --> 00:03:06,716
At exactly k equals one, the core is sustained critical.

50
00:03:06,716 --> 00:03:10,878
But if k exceeds one by even a fraction of a percent,

51
00:03:10,878 --> 00:03:15,040
prompt neutrons alone cause the reaction to run away exponentially within microseconds.

52
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:19,926
Those who touched the plutonium core reported an eerie physical sensation: the

53
00:03:19,926 --> 00:03:22,537
metal was distinctly warm to the touch.

54
00:03:22,537 --> 00:03:27,013
Because trillions of alpha particles constantly bombarded the crystal lattice from within,

55
00:03:27,013 --> 00:03:29,624
the core generated heat like a living,

56
00:03:29,624 --> 00:03:34,100
mechanical heart, radiating approximately fourteen watts of thermal power day and night.

57
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:38,113
Safety protocols were casual to the point of absurdity.

58
00:03:38,113 --> 00:03:41,334
Scientists frequently worked late into the night alone,

59
00:03:41,334 --> 00:03:46,166
accompanied only by armed military guards who had no scientific training whatsoever.

60
00:03:46,166 --> 00:03:50,997
The young researchers were trusted completely with materials worth millions of dollars

61
00:03:50,997 --> 00:03:53,010
and capable of catastrophic devastation.

62
00:03:53,410 --> 00:03:57,956
By late August 1945, dozens of successful assembly tests had been performed

63
00:03:57,956 --> 00:04:01,365
without a single hitch. Overconfidence began to replace caution.

64
00:04:01,365 --> 00:04:04,395
The scientists believed they had mastered the beast.

65
00:04:04,395 --> 00:04:08,941
But the dragon was merely waiting for a single slip of human

66
00:04:08,941 --> 00:04:09,320
fingers.

67
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:12,792
On the night of August 21st, 1945,

68
00:04:12,792 --> 00:04:18,058
twenty-four-year-old physicist Harry Daghlian walked into the Omega Site laboratory shortly after

69
00:04:18,058 --> 00:04:21,130
nine o'clock. Working completely against official regulations,

70
00:04:21,130 --> 00:04:24,641
he decided to perform a manual assembly alone,

71
00:04:24,641 --> 00:04:29,030
accompanied only by a seated military guard named Robert Hemmerly.

72
00:04:29,430 --> 00:04:34,004
Daghlian built a four-sided wall of tungsten carbide bricks around the plutonium

73
00:04:34,004 --> 00:04:38,577
sphere. As he carefully hoisted the final four-point-four kilogram brick to place

74
00:04:38,577 --> 00:04:42,007
it directly above the core, the radiation counter screamed.

75
00:04:42,007 --> 00:04:46,200
Daghlian flinched. The heavy, polished brick slipped from his sweaty fingers.

76
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:50,085
The brick crashed directly onto the top of the core.

77
00:04:50,085 --> 00:04:52,873
Instantly, the assembly went prompt critical. A brilliant,

78
00:04:52,873 --> 00:04:56,010
ethereal blast of blue-violet light erupted into the room,

79
00:04:56,010 --> 00:05:00,192
accompanied by an intense wave of radiant heat that washed over Daghlian’s

80
00:05:00,192 --> 00:05:02,980
face and chest like an open blast furnace.

81
00:05:03,380 --> 00:05:06,003
Reacting on pure survival instinct, Daghlian lunged forward.

82
00:05:06,003 --> 00:05:09,282
He tried to shove the fallen brick off the core,

83
00:05:09,282 --> 00:05:11,906
but it was too slippery. In sheer panic,

84
00:05:11,906 --> 00:05:15,841
he used his bare hands to violently knock down the entire incandescent

85
00:05:15,841 --> 00:05:19,120
stack of heavy bricks, scattering them across the wooden floor.

86
00:05:19,530 --> 00:05:23,080
The entire criticality burst lasted barely a few seconds,

87
00:05:23,080 --> 00:05:27,814
but the invisible damage was catastrophic. Daghlian had absorbed an estimated five

88
00:05:27,814 --> 00:05:31,759
hundred and ten rems of massive neutron and gamma radiation.

89
00:05:31,759 --> 00:05:35,310
His right hand alone, which had dismantled the stack,

90
00:05:35,310 --> 00:05:38,860
absorbed over twenty thousand rads of blistering thermal exposure.

91
00:05:39,250 --> 00:05:42,127
Within hours, severe nausea and swelling set in.

92
00:05:42,127 --> 00:05:45,003
Daghlian was rushed to the Los Alamos hospital,

93
00:05:45,003 --> 00:05:49,319
where his blistered skin turned necrotic and his organs steadily shut down.

94
00:05:49,319 --> 00:05:52,195
He died twenty-five days later, on September 15th.

95
00:05:52,195 --> 00:05:56,510
From that dark day forward, the metal sphere was rechristened: The Demon

96
00:05:56,510 --> 00:05:56,870
Core.

97
00:05:57,270 --> 00:06:00,263
Following Daghlian's death, safety guidelines were drafted,

98
00:06:00,263 --> 00:06:05,395
but the culture of bravado persisted. Leadership of the critical assembly group

99
00:06:05,395 --> 00:06:08,388
passed to thirty-five-year-old Canadian physicist Louis Slotin.

100
00:06:08,388 --> 00:06:11,809
Brilliant, charismatic, and fearless, Slotin wore cowboy boots,

101
00:06:11,809 --> 00:06:16,940
blue jeans, and lived for the intoxicating thrill of mastering chain reactions.

102
00:06:17,340 --> 00:06:21,970
On May 21st, 1946, Slotin was preparing to hand over his duties

103
00:06:21,970 --> 00:06:26,214
before the core was shipped to Bikini Atoll for atomic tests.

104
00:06:26,214 --> 00:06:30,844
Seven other scientists and observers gathered inside the Omega Site laboratory to

105
00:06:30,844 --> 00:06:35,474
watch Slotin demonstrate what he casually referred to as his trademark criticality

106
00:06:35,474 --> 00:06:35,860
procedure.

107
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:39,046
This time, the reflector was not heavy bricks,

108
00:06:39,046 --> 00:06:42,528
but two nested, nine-inch hollow hemispheres made of pure beryllium.

109
00:06:42,528 --> 00:06:46,706
The bottom hemisphere sat in a metal cradle holding the plutonium core.

110
00:06:46,706 --> 00:06:50,884
Slotin’s task was to slowly lower the upper dome over the core

111
00:06:50,884 --> 00:06:53,670
to measure neutron multiplication through the remaining gap.

112
00:06:54,070 --> 00:06:58,672
Official laboratory protocol strictly mandated using solid wooden wedges or metal shims

113
00:06:58,672 --> 00:07:02,123
to ensure the upper dome could never completely close.

114
00:07:02,123 --> 00:07:06,724
But Slotin found wedges clumsy. Instead, he casually propped the upper hemisphere

115
00:07:06,724 --> 00:07:11,326
open using only the blade of a standard two-inch flathead screwdriver held

116
00:07:11,326 --> 00:07:12,860
in his right hand.

117
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:15,731
Colleagues in the room grew visibly tense.

118
00:07:15,731 --> 00:07:18,909
Physicist Raemer Schreiber looked away for a split second.

119
00:07:18,909 --> 00:07:23,146
At three twenty in the afternoon, Slotin’s screwdriver blade suddenly slipped one

120
00:07:23,146 --> 00:07:27,029
millimeter on the polished beryllium lip. With a heavy metallic click,

121
00:07:27,029 --> 00:07:30,560
the upper dome dropped shut, completely sealing the plutonium core.

122
00:07:30,970 --> 00:07:35,755
In less than five hundred microseconds, the sealed core exploded into prompt

123
00:07:35,755 --> 00:07:40,541
supercriticality. A blinding flash of blue Cherenkov light surged through the room,

124
00:07:40,541 --> 00:07:44,130
accompanied by an instantaneous wave of searing thermal heat.

125
00:07:44,130 --> 00:07:48,915
Slotin instantly tasted a sharp, sour metallic flavor on his tongue—the hallmark

126
00:07:48,915 --> 00:07:50,510
of massive cellular ionization.

127
00:07:50,910 --> 00:07:53,158
With superhuman reflexes, Slotin did not run.

128
00:07:53,158 --> 00:07:57,012
He seized the scorching beryllium dome with his bare left hand and

129
00:07:57,012 --> 00:08:00,545
threw it violently to the floor, instantly terminating the chain reaction.

130
00:08:00,545 --> 00:08:03,436
Shielding the other seven men with his own body,

131
00:08:03,436 --> 00:08:06,326
Slotin calmly turned to his colleagues and said: 'Well,

132
00:08:06,326 --> 00:08:07,290
that does it.'

133
00:08:07,690 --> 00:08:10,677
Slotin knew immediately that his life was forfeit.

134
00:08:10,677 --> 00:08:14,785
Remaining astonishingly clear-headed, he ordered everyone to stay frozen in place.

135
00:08:14,785 --> 00:08:17,398
Using a piece of white blackboard chalk,

136
00:08:17,398 --> 00:08:21,879
Slotin marked the exact spot on the wooden floor where every single

137
00:08:21,879 --> 00:08:26,360
person had stood, enabling medical physicists to calculate their precise radiation exposure.

138
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:31,802
Slotin had absorbed an astronomical dose of one thousand rads of neutron

139
00:08:31,802 --> 00:08:35,584
and gamma radiation—far beyond any known medical survival threshold.

140
00:08:35,584 --> 00:08:39,366
Over the next nine days at Los Alamos Hospital,

141
00:08:39,366 --> 00:08:43,568
his gastrointestinal tract disintegrated and his white blood cells vanished.

142
00:08:43,568 --> 00:08:46,509
Louis Slotin passed away on May 30th,

143
00:08:46,509 --> 00:08:47,770
1946, aged thirty-five.

144
00:08:48,170 --> 00:08:52,979
Because Slotin's body had physically absorbed the brunt of the neutron blast,

145
00:08:52,979 --> 00:08:57,789
all seven other men survived. However, the phantom of radiation followed them

146
00:08:57,789 --> 00:09:01,396
for decades. Several developed premature cataracts, chronic blood disorders,

147
00:09:01,396 --> 00:09:04,201
and psychological scars that never fully healed,

148
00:09:04,201 --> 00:09:08,610
serving as living testaments to that fateful afternoon in Pajarito Canyon.

149
00:09:09,010 --> 00:09:11,890
The second disaster was the final straw.

150
00:09:11,890 --> 00:09:16,827
Laboratory director Norris Bradbury and General Leslie Groves immediately issued an absolute,

151
00:09:16,827 --> 00:09:19,706
permanent ban on all manual criticality experiments.

152
00:09:19,706 --> 00:09:23,820
Never again would a human being touch a fissile core.

153
00:09:23,820 --> 00:09:28,757
From that moment on, all criticality tests were conducted entirely by remote-controlled

154
00:09:28,757 --> 00:09:29,580
robotic manipulators.

155
00:09:29,970 --> 00:09:32,689
What happened to the Demon Core itself?

156
00:09:32,689 --> 00:09:36,572
Initially slated for the Able nuclear test at Bikini Atoll,

157
00:09:36,572 --> 00:09:40,068
officials delayed its detonation to verify its physical integrity.

158
00:09:40,068 --> 00:09:43,174
In July 1946, the core was melted down,

159
00:09:43,174 --> 00:09:47,835
recooled, and reconstituted into new weapon pits that were later detonated over

160
00:09:47,835 --> 00:09:49,000
the Pacific Ocean.

161
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:53,642
The Demon Core claimed two brilliant lives not because the laws of

162
00:09:53,642 --> 00:09:57,531
quantum physics failed, but because human confidence momentarily triumphed over caution.

163
00:09:57,531 --> 00:10:00,006
In the dawn of the atomic age,

164
00:10:00,006 --> 00:10:03,895
it proved that when dealing with cosmic forces trapped inside metal,

165
00:10:03,895 --> 00:10:07,430
a single millimeter is the difference between life and death.
